Tires are the foundation of a heavy-duty truck’s performance and safety. A
blowout at highway speed is expensive at best and catastrophic at worst. Proper
tire maintenance prevents most of them, and it also extends tread life, improves
fuel efficiency and lowers operating cost.

Here are seven checks worth holding to.

## 1. Maintain proper tire pressure

Under-inflated and over-inflated tires are a leading cause of blowouts and
uneven wear, and both cost fuel.

- Check pressure daily with a gauge you trust.
- Follow the manufacturer’s recommended pressure, from the manual or the door
  placard.
- Adjust for load weight and weather.
- Consider automatic tire inflation systems (ATIS) or tire pressure monitoring
  systems (TPMS) for pressure you can see in real time.

## 2. Inspect tread depth regularly

Worn tread reduces traction, lengthens stopping distance and raises the risk of
hydroplaning.

- Measure with a tread depth gauge at several points across the tire.
- Replace at 4/32″ on steer tires and 2/32″ on drive and trailer tires, per DOT
  regulations.
- Treat uneven wear as a symptom: it usually means alignment or suspension.

## 3. Rotate tires on a schedule

Rotation evens out wear, which is what gets a tire to its rated life.

- Rotate steer, drive and trailer tires every 30,000 to 50,000 miles, or as the
  manufacturer specifies.
- Follow the rotation pattern for the configuration — dual tires and wide-base
  singles are not the same.
- Cupping or feathering means the suspension or alignment needs attention first.

## 4. Align wheels and balance tires

Misaligned wheels scrub tread, add vibration and burn fuel.

- Check alignment every six months, and after potholes, curbs or rough ground.
- Balance whenever new tires go on.
- Treat pulling to one side as an alignment fault until proven otherwise.

## 5. Avoid overloading your tires

Overloading overheats a tire, and heat is what separates tread from casing.

- Work inside the Gross Vehicle Weight Rating (GVWR) and the tire load index.
- Distribute weight evenly across the axles.
- Use a weigh station or onboard load sensors rather than an estimate.

## 6. Check for damage and sidewall cracks

Small cuts, bulges and cracks become failures if they are left.

- Look for punctures, embedded debris and cracks in the sidewall and tread.
- Inspect valve stems for leaks and dry rot.
- Treat separation or bubbling as internal damage — that tire is finished.

## 7. Use quality tires and retreads wisely

Good tires cost less per mile, which is the number that matters.

- Choose a high ply rating and reinforced sidewalls for the duty cycle.
- Retread drive and trailer tires only when the casing is genuinely sound.
- Buy from manufacturers that meet FMCSA safety standards.

## Conclusion

Tire maintenance is the cheapest downtime prevention available. Watch pressure,
measure tread, keep the geometry right and stay inside the load rating, and a
fleet saves money on tires and fuel at the same time.

